A statement `if` runs a block when its condition is `true`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    if ready {
        io.line("ready")
    else
        io.line("waiting")
    }
]

### Contract

- The ordinary condition must resolve to `Bool`.
- `else` is optional.
- The `if` body and optional `else` body are child scopes.
- A single pair of braces encloses both branches. A bare `else` separates their scopes.
- The closing brace completes the conditional. An `else` after it needs an enclosing conditional.
- Statement `if` is non-exhaustive when no `else` is written.
- A statement branch does not produce a value merely because its body contains expressions.

Block and branch placement follows @../language-overview/#blocks-and-statements (Blocks and statements).

### Compile-time-known statement conditions

An ordinary Boolean conditional also handles compile-time-known conditions. The frontend validates both branches before a known Bool selects the executable body. The selected lexical scope remains intact, and inactive downstream compiler work is absent. An unknown `Bool` lowers normally.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    enabled #= false

    if enabled {
        perform_optional_work()
    }
]

A `$config`-marked Bool input is one source of the same folded value. It uses this ordinary
folded-value path rather than introducing config-specific branch syntax.

### No statement-level `else if`

A same-line `else if` chain is not syntax.

Use a nested `if` inside the `else` body:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    if first_condition {
        io.line("first")
    else
        if second_condition {
            io.line("second")
        else
            io.line("fallback")
        }
    }
]

Use a full match when one value is being compared against several patterns.

### Option statement capture

A statement can test an optional value and bind its present payload:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    if maybe_name is |name| {
        io.line(name)
    }
]

It can also test for absence:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    if maybe_name is none {
        io.line("missing")
    }
]

Without `else`, the unmatched case does nothing.
